  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "After getting lost in Hollywood, ibti decided to give you yet another problem related to permutations.\n\nConsider a permutation PPP of length 2⋅N2\\cdot N2⋅N.\n\nAn array AAA of size NNN is generated using the permutation PPP, such that the ithi^{th}ith element of the array is a **pair** having value, Ai=(Pi mod N,P(i+N) mod N)A_i = (P_i \\bmod N, P_{(i+N)} \\bmod N)Ai​=(Pi​modN,P(i+N)​modN).\n\nThe permutation PPP (of length 2⋅N2\\cdot N2⋅N) is called NNN-beautiful, if, all elements of the array AAA are **distinct**.\n\nGiven integers KKK and MMM, your task is to count the number of iii-beautiful permutations for each 1≤i≤K1 \\le i \\le K1≤i≤K.\n\nSince the answers can be large, print them modulo MMM.\n\n\nInput Format\nThe first and only line of the input contains two space-separated integers KKK, MMM - the parameters defined in the statement.\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nPrint KKK space-separated integers, the ithi^{th}ith integer will denote the number of iii-beautiful permutations modulo MMM.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤K≤2⋅1051 \\le K \\le 2 \\cdot 10^51≤K≤2⋅105",
      "107+19≤M≤109+710^7 + 19 \\le M \\le 10^9+7107+19≤M≤109+7",
      "It is guaranteed that MMM is a prime number.",
      "Subtasks",
      "**Subtask 1 (50 points):** 1≤K≤1031 \\leq K \\leq 10^31≤K≤103",
      "**Subtask 2 (50 points):** No further constraints."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "8 269696969",
        "output": "2 16 576 34560 3110400 142258231 78756849 13872609",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nAll permutations of length 222 are 111-beautiful, thus the first value we print is 2!=22! = 22!=2.\nThere are 161616 222-beautiful permutations out of the 242424 possible permutations. The 888 non-beautiful permutations are: {1,3,2,4},{1,3,4,2},{2,4,1,3},{2,4,3,1},{3,1,2,4},{3,1,4,2},{4,2,1,3},{4,2,3,1}\\{1, 3, 2, 4\\}, \\{1, 3, 4, 2\\}, \\{2, 4, 1, 3\\}, \\{2, 4, 3, 1\\}, \\{3, 1, 2, 4\\}, \\{3, 1, 4, 2\\}, \\{4, 2, 1, 3\\}, \\{4, 2, 3, 1\\}{1,3,2,4},{1,3,4,2},{2,4,1,3},{2,4,3,1},{3,1,2,4},{3,1,4,2},{4,2,1,3},{4,2,3,1}\nFor example, the permutation {1,3,2,4}\\{1, 3, 2, 4\\}{1,3,2,4} is not beautiful as A=[(1 mod 2,2 mod 2),(3 mod 2,4 mod 2)]=[(1,0),(1,0)]A = [(1 \\bmod 2, 2 \\bmod 2), (3 \\bmod 2, 4 \\bmod 2)] = [(1, 0), (1, 0)]A=[(1mod2,2mod2),(3mod2,4mod2)]=[(1,0),(1,0)]. Both elements of the array AAA are same."
      }
    ]
  },
